From 34ec8a62bfb657ef441e3a20292417cf81b0d9e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:15:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 84/84] avformat/aviobuf: pad the dynamic buffer after the last
 byte

avio_close_dyn_buf() appends AV_INPUT_BUFFER_PADDING_SIZE zero bytes and
returns the size minus that padding. The padding is written at the
current write position, which is the end of the data only if the muxer
left it there.

Muxers that seek back in write_trailer() to patch a field in the header
and do not seek forward again (flv, aiff, caf and asf, for example) leave
the position in the middle of the data. The 64 zero bytes of padding then
overwrote the output right after the patched field, and the returned
size was 64 bytes too short, so the last 64 bytes were cut off.

Reproduced on current master by muxing the same input with
avio_open_dyn_buf() and with avio_open() and comparing the results:

  flv   119467 bytes to a file, 119403 bytes from the dynamic buffer
  aiff  176454 bytes to a file, 176390 bytes from the dynamic buffer
  caf   176492 bytes to a file, 176428 bytes from the dynamic buffer
  asf    19644 bytes to a file,  19580 bytes from the dynamic buffer

Flush what is still buffered, move the position to the end of the data
and only then write the padding. Muxers that finish at the end of the
data, such as mp4, matroska and mpegts, are not affected, and the output
is byte for byte the same as before for them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FZ4G6798n6Ua4qu6xwWu3S
---
 libavformat/aviobuf.c | 15 ++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/libavformat/aviobuf.c b/libavformat/aviobuf.c
index 43431381b9..5b6a00f3ef 100644
--- a/libavformat/aviobuf.c
+++ b/libavformat/aviobuf.c
@@ -1386,15 +1386,24 @@ int avio_close_dyn_buf(AVIOContext *s, uint8_t **pbuffer)
         return 0;
     }
 
+    /* Write out what is still buffered before looking at the position:
+     * a muxer that seeked back to patch its header can leave its last
+     * bytes there. */
+    avio_flush(s);
+
+    d = s->opaque;
+
     /* don't attempt to pad fixed-size packet buffers */
     if (!s->max_packet_size) {
+        /* The muxer may have left the write position in the middle of the
+         * data, e.g. after updating the duration in the header. The padding
+         * belongs after the last byte, not over the data at that position. */
+        d->pos = d->size;
         ffio_fill(s, 0, AV_INPUT_BUFFER_PADDING_SIZE);
         padding = AV_INPUT_BUFFER_PADDING_SIZE;
+        avio_flush(s);
     }
 
-    avio_flush(s);
-
-    d = s->opaque;
     *pbuffer = d->buffer;
     size = d->size;
 
